is now a step closer to ousting Israel's longest-serving premier.\n\nActual Article Body:\nIsraeli President Isaac 
Herzog said Saturday night that he was giving opposition candidate Gadi Eisenkot the first opportunity to form a 
governing coalition following elections on Oct. 27.\n\nEisenkot, a former military chief who ran as the main 
challenger to Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, is now a step closer to ousting Israel's longest-serving 
premier.\n\n\"This is the time to establish a national emergency government. I will do anything possible to prove us 
worthy,\" Eisenkot posted on his X account just after the presidential announcement.\n\nHe has up to six weeks to form 
a coalition, a challenging task in a deeply divided country reeling from the Oct. 7, 2023, Hamas-led attacks and the 
three years of wars that have followed in Gaza and beyond.\n\nIn discussions with the president this week, 
representatives from most of Israel's main parties recommended Eisenkot be tasked with assembling a new government. 
But putting together the parliamentary majority needed remains fraught and any possible coalition would require 
candidates to make tough compromises that could outrage their own voters.\n\nNetanyahu and his allies lost their 
majority in parliament in last month's elections. But the bloc led by Eisenkot also didn't win a majority. That left 
Arab-led parties, which made historic gains, as potential but politically fraught kingmakers.\n\nAn Arab party has 
been part of a governing coalition just once in Israel's history. Forming such an alliance could be especially 
divisive for Eisenkot after more than three years of war in Gaza.\n\nNetanyahu has been on a campaign to head off any 
alliance between Eisenkot and the Arab-led parties, including unsubstantiated claims that Arab parties \"stole\" the 
elections. Shortly before Herzog's announcement, he called for a \"national unity government\" in a video statement 
and warned against \"hastily swearing in a government with terrorist supporters\" �?" language he uses to refer to the 
Arab-led parties.\n\nNetanyahu and his allies have repeatedly portrayed the Arab-led parties as a threat to national 
security, while Eisenkot has indicated a willingness to partner with them under certain conditions, including calling 
Hamas a terrorist group that needs to be destroyed.\n\nEisenkot and Netanyahu, whose Likud is the single largest party 
in parliament, could seek to create a broad unity government with major Jewish parties. However, the men have big 
differences over many issues that will be difficult to bridge. That includes Netanyahu's...",
