    "text": "Stated Claim / Post Context:\nLetter: Who benefits from Utah�?Ts crackdown on prediction 
markets?\n\nLetter: Who benefits from Utah�?Ts crackdown on prediction markets?\n\nActual Article Body:\n(Francisco 
Kjolseth | The Salt Lake Tribune) Utah Attorney General Derek Brown attends a meeting of the Capitol Preservation 
Board at the Utah Capitol on Thursday, Oct. 1, 2026.\n\nComment\n\nAdd as Preferred Source\n\nIs Utah Attorney General 
Derek Brown protecting consumers from prediction markets, or will his crackdown do nothing other than limit 
marketplace competition from well-funded industry interests?\n\nAccording to an Oct. 29 Tribune story, Brown has 
accepted tens of thousands of dollars from companies with stakes in hamstringing their competitors. The biggest check, 
$50,000, came from Las Vegas Sands, a brick-and-mortar casino giant that has very publicly challenged its online 
competition for more than a decade. Its check arrived just weeks after Brown�?Ts legal fight with Kalshi, the largest 
prediction market, began. He also received $20,000 from Kalshi�?Ts rivals DraftKings and PrizePicks, along with an 
Underdog executive, since 2025.\n\nNone of that proves anything improper, of course, and Brown�?Ts campaign says 
donations don�?Tt influence him. But Utahns still have every right to question their attorney general�?Ts decision on 
policy grounds.\n\nAs an economist and longtime student of regulation, I know Congress gave the Commodity Futures 
Trading Commission authority over federally regulated derivatives exchanges for a reason. But as with most regulatory 
initiatives, politics and special interests enter to shape regulatory outcomes.\n\nA prediction market is a single 
national marketplace. A trader in Salt Lake City and a trader in Boston buy and sell the same contract, at the same 
price, on the same exchange. Only a national regulator can see the entire market, which is what it takes to catch 
manipulation and insider trading. That�?Ts why we don�?Tt let each state write its own stock market rules.\n\nThe CFTC 
considers the contracts federally regulated swaps under its exclusive jurisdiction, and it has now sued Utah for 
stepping on its regulatory turf.\n\nBrown should ensure that his prediction-market crackdown helps consumers, whom the 
CFTC and the FTC are already charged with protecting, and not the very gaming interests banned from operating in 
Utah.\n\nThis site is protected by reCAPTCHA and the Google Privacy Policy and Terms of Service apply.\n\nWilliam F. 
Shughart II, Logan\n\nSubmit a letter to the editor\n\nsltrib.com is now free to access �?" no subscription required. 
