Body:\nEmergency\n\nBackstory\n\nNewsletters\n\n�,�-ؑ-�-�\n\nBERITA BAHASA INDONESIA\n\nTOK PISIN\n\nSport\n\nBy 
Simon Smale\n\nTopic:England Soccer\n\nSpot the ball? You'd be hard pushed to see anything at Moss Lane in these 
conditions. (Supplied: TNT Sports)\n\nDoes a defeat count if nobody saw it?\n\nAltrincham fans may well be wondering 
that this weekend, after fog descended upon its FA Cup first round tie against Notts County at Moss Lane in 
Manchester.\n\nLeague One side Notts County, featuring Australian former Sydney FC striker Luka Smyth, had taken a 
first half lead before Manchester-based Altrincham, of the fifth-tier National League, hit the front.\n\nA late 
equaliser for Nottingham-based County, who won the FA Cup back in the 1893/94 season, forced extra time.\n\nHowever, 
by that stage a heavy fog had enveloped the entire stadium, meaning extra time and penalties were played in scarcely 
believable conditions.\n\nIt made one slightly pity the commentators.\n\n\"I don't want to worry anyone �?" we can't 
see those dugouts at the moment because of the mist,\" Steve Bower said on the BBC in the closing stages of normal 
time, before fatefully adding that \"it's getting heavier\".\n\n\"We will try our best to let you know who has got the 
ball.\"\n\nFormer Notts County striker Mark Stallard said on the BBC Radio Nottingham call that \"We're looking around 
�?� no-one knows where the ball is.\"\n\n\"We genuinely cannot see the linesman or the players on the far side. It's 
slightly farcical having to commentate for radio listeners.\"\n\nAt least he was only supposed to be 
summarising.\n\n\"I can just about see the bright white numbers on their shirts, but I am struggling to see what is 
happening on the far side of the pitch,\" his play-by-play caller Adam Hassell said.\n\nThe scenes were reminiscent of 
another famous foggy incident involving legendary Charlton goalkeeper Sam Bartram.\n\nOn Christmas Day, 1937, a London 
pea souper rolled into Stamford Bridge and completely covered the field.\n\nIn his autobiography, Bartram recalled 
seeing \"fewer and fewer figures as we attacked steadily\" over the course of the match, right up until a policeman 
appeared on the edge of his penalty area.\n\n\"'What on earth are you doing here?' he gasped. 'The game was stopped a 
quarter of an hour ago. The field's completely empty,'\" Bartram wrote.\n\n\"When I groped my way to the 
dressing-room, the rest of the Charlton team, already out of the bath and in their civvies, were convulsed with 
laughter.\"\n\nFortunately, nothing like that happened in...",
