    "text": "Stated Claim / Post Context:\nAussie's heroic Paris Masters run ends at the hands of rising French 
star\n\nAfter downing Novak Djokovic earlier in the week, Australia's Rinky Hijikata's heroic Paris Masters run 
finally comes to an end.\n\nActual Article Body:\nEmergency\n\nBackstory\n\nNewsletters\n\n�,�-ؑ-�-�\n\nBERITA 
BAHASA INDONESIA\n\nTOK PISIN\n\nSport\n\nTopic:Tennis\n\nAustralia's Rinky Hijikata fell to France's Luca van Assche 
at the Paris Masters. (Getty Images: Sport News)\n\nAustralia's Rinky Hijikata lost to Luca Van Assche in three sets 
to end his heroic Paris Masters run.\n\nHijikata had earlier stunned tennis superstar Novak Djokovic to set up his 
first Masters 1000 quarter final.\n\nThe Paris run makes Hijikata Australia's second-ranked men's player, behind Alex 
de Minaur.\n\nRinky Hijikata has finally been awakened from his dream week at the Paris Masters as a new young French 
tennis hero ended the Australian's bid to reach his first big-time tennis semi-final.\n\nFor the third match running, 
Hijikata, who came from behindAto defeat Novak Djokovic in the second round, battled back from a set down with some 
scintillating tennis as he quieted the home fans cheering for their new hope, Luca Van Assche.\n\nBut it was the 
22-year-old Paris-based Van Assche, also playing in his first Masters 1000 quarter-final, who rose to new heights as 
he came from 2-0 down in the final set to prevail 6-4, 6-7 (4-7), 7-5 after a memorable two-and-three-quarter hour 
duel.\n\nIt meant the end of what felt like a major breakthrough week for the 25-year-old Sydneysider Hijikata, who 
looked early in that final set as if he was going to win his sixth match in seven days and become the first qualifier 
to reach the last-four at the Paris Masters in nine years.\n\nUltimately, he was defeated by a young player who said 
he felt inspired by the crowd as he struggled physically in the deciding set.\n\n\"The crowd were so important for me, 
supporting me so much in the tough moments, and that's why I played my best tennis,\" said Belgian-born Van Assche, 
who becomes the 12th Frenchman to make the semi-finals in Paris and will next tackle American Ben 
Shelton.\n\nHijikata's consolation will be to start next week as Australia's number two man behind Alex de Minaur as 
he enters the world's top-60.\n\nHijikata certainly continued with that fearless, aggressive streak in the second set 
tiebreak against Van Assche, maintaining his fine form by then earning an immediate break in the third set.\n\nBut Van 
Assche is one of the most highly regarded young talents in the game, and his defensive prowess helped him turn the 
tide in the final set with some extraordinary wall-like resilience that kept forcing Hijikata to play one shot too 
many.\n\nThird seed Shelton, bombing down 19 aces, had...",
