sensors, sleep logs and wrist-worn monitors.\n\nNeither group showed significant improvements in blood sugar. Both 
groups lost weight, but those receiving sleep coaching did not lose significantly more than those receiving lifestyle 
education alone.\n\nParticipants who received sleep coaching did report better sleep quality and less daytime 
drowsiness, and they gradually began going to bed earlier. (iStock)\n\n\"While we expected that extending sleep would 
improve glucose levels and weight loss in adults with prediabetes who sleep for shorter periods, this wasn't the case 
in our study,\" Malone said.\n\nParticipants who received sleep coaching did report better sleep quality and less 
