Los Angeles on Nov. 24, 2022.\n                \n                    \n                    Damian Dovarganes/AP\n      
              \n                \n                hide caption\n\nAfter years of increasing to record-high levels, the 
homeless population in the U.S. dropped more than 6% in 2025. That amounts to roughly 49,000 fewer people without 
housing. It's the second year in a row that homelessness has declined, with a smaller drop in 2024.\n\n\"President 
Trump's actions to secure the border, get criminal illegals off our streets, and restore safety to our communities 
drove a historic decrease in homelessness,\" Housing and Urban Development Secretary Scott Turner said in a press 
release announcing the results of an annual count in communities around the country. The agency had not yet posted a 
detailed report.\n\nA second independent analysis based on the same count, but commissioned by the advocacy group 
National Alliance to End Homelessness, found a similar decline but credited different reasons for it.\n\n\"It's good 
news that more people are getting housed, and that the programs that are funded by Congress are working,\" said CEO 
Ann Oliva.\n\nBoth analyses find there's still nearly 700,000 people in the U.S. living in shelters or on the streets, 
significantly more than before the COVID-19 pandemic.\n\nThe Trump administration cited its changes in immigration 
policy as a key factor in the decline. During the Biden administration, New York, Chicago, Boston and Denver all saw a 
surge in asylum seekers who turned up at homeless shelters. \"That's also the four cities that have driven half of the 
drop most recently,\" said Devon Kurtz, Public Safety Policy Director at the Cicero Institute, an administration ally 
on homelessness policy.\n\nKurtz also pointed to declines in cities like San Francisco, which is moving to support 
more sober-living options, housing that bans drugs and alcohol. But critics say that can send more people back to the 
street if they relapse, which is common.\n\nThe Trump administration argues that the requirement to be drug-free would 
help more people achieve recovery, and wants to expand such programs. In fact, the administration wants to overhaul 
several billion dollars in homelessness spending and shift it toward...",
